Subject: [PATCH] kbase: create the CSF queue group at HIGH priority

Purpose: a group created at the default (MEDIUM) priority stays in the scheduler's rotation,
 and kbase's off-slot heap-reclaim shrinker can empty the tiler heap of a group that was taken
 off its slot. Under a heavy scene the next pass would then find the heap gone and kbase
 terminate the group (CS_UNRECOVERABLE), after which the vendor kernel can panic while dumping
 the blocked KCPU queue (seen on a Lenovo TB373FU, Mali-G615, mali_kbase_mt6897_r44). HIGH
 resists the rotation. This is the idea zenithblue's notes give; it is not yet shown on this
 driver (ticket #23 checks it with the 30000-fish stress test). Only MEDIUM is rewritten, so
 any other priority a caller asks for is untouched. If the kernel refuses HIGH the group is
 created again at the priority asked for, so a phone that caps HIGH keeps its driver.
Source/Reference: zenithblue-oss/panvk-kbase-android (MIT scaffolding), which creates its
 groups at kbase priority 0 (HIGH); re-written for this port, no code copied.
Generated against: PANVK_REV 58f2c55854bd48b18df756edb11f187af9cd2fde (leo-snczki/mali-panvk).
 If a PANVK_REV bump stops this applying, rebase it; the build stops rather than ship without it.
Tested: HIGH is accepted on the TB373FU by an unprivileged app (seen in that driver's log).
Not changed: the per-queue priority passed to CS_QUEUE_REGISTER (its scale within a group is
 not confirmed), the tiler heap size, the OOM exception flag (ticket #22).

diff -ruN a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
--- a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c	2026-11-04 12:19:02.000000000 +0000
+++ b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c	2026-11-09 15:53:59.010339191 +0000
@@ -2545,21 +2545,41 @@
    for (unsigned q = 0; q < nr_queues; q++)
       g->seqno[q * 8] = 0;
 
-   union kbase_ioctl_cs_queue_group_create gc = {
-      .in = {
-         .tiler_mask = 1,
-         .fragment_mask = fragment_mask,
-         .compute_mask = compute_mask,
-         .cs_min = nr_queues,
-         .priority = priority,
-         .tiler_max = 1,
-         .fragment_max = max_fragment,
-         .compute_max = max_compute,
-      },
-   };
-   if (pan_kmod_ioctl(dev->fd, KBASE_IOCTL_CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE, &gc)) {
-      mesa_loge("kbase: CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE failed (err=%d)", errno);
-      goto err_free_seq;
+   /* Tessera: a group asked for at the default (MEDIUM) priority is created HIGH first. The idea,
+    * from zenithblue's panvk-kbase-android notes, is that a group that stays in the scheduler's
+    * rotation is taken off its firmware slot between passes and kbase's off-slot heap-reclaim
+    * shrinker can then empty its tiler heap, so that under a heavy scene the next pass finds the
+    * heap gone and the group is terminated; HIGH resists that rotation. Not yet shown on this
+    * driver (it is checked with the stress test). A kbase that refuses HIGH (it can cap it) must
+    * not cost the driver its device, so the group is then created at the priority asked for. */
+   const uint8_t requested = priority;
+   if (priority == PAN_KMOD_KBASE_CSF_PRIORITY_MEDIUM)
+      priority = PAN_KMOD_KBASE_CSF_PRIORITY_HIGH;
+
+   union kbase_ioctl_cs_queue_group_create gc;
+   for (;;) {
+      /* Built afresh each time: a failed ioctl may have written into the union. */
+      gc = (union kbase_ioctl_cs_queue_group_create){
+         .in = {
+            .tiler_mask = 1,
+            .fragment_mask = fragment_mask,
+            .compute_mask = compute_mask,
+            .cs_min = nr_queues,
+            .priority = priority,
+            .tiler_max = 1,
+            .fragment_max = max_fragment,
+            .compute_max = max_compute,
+         },
+      };
+      if (!pan_kmod_ioctl(dev->fd, KBASE_IOCTL_CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE, &gc))
+         break;
+      if (priority == requested) {
+         mesa_loge("kbase: CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE failed (err=%d)", errno);
+         goto err_free_seq;
+      }
+      mesa_logw("kbase: queue group at priority %u refused (err=%d), using %u", priority,
+                errno, requested);
+      priority = requested;
    }
    g->handle = gc.out.group_handle;
 
